  "worked_solution": "The particle's velocity vector is given by the derivative of its position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{7}{t+1}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{5t}{{t}^{2}+1})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{7}{(t+1)^{2}},\\frac{5-5{t}^{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{2}}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the velocity when $t=2,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\u27e8-\\frac{7}{(2+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{5-5(2)^{2}}{((2)^{2}+1)^{2}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-\\frac{7}{9},\\,-\\frac{3}{5}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$",
